<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!--
      Transitive pinning. Without this, a vulnerable package pulled in indirectly can only be
      patched by adding a direct PackageReference to it, which lies about the dependency
      graph: the project does not use the package, it merely needs a newer copy of it than
      its parent asked for. With this enabled, a PackageVersion entry alone is enough to
      raise the floor, and no fake direct reference is required.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ==== Direct dependencies ==== -->

  <ItemGroup Label="Data access">
    <!--
      10.0.11 floors SQLitePCLRaw at 2.1.12. Bumping this parent is preferable to pinning the
      SQLitePCLRaw packages individually: they ship as a matched set (core, bundle, provider,
      lib) and Microsoft.Data.Sqlite is what knows which combination it was tested against.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!--
      Dapper materialises rows by building a dynamic method per query shape on first use. That
      is runtime IL emission, so this assembly is neither trimmable nor ahead-of-time
      compilable, and it says so rather than claiming otherwise.

      Dapper.AOT was referenced to remove exactly that, and did nothing. Its generator
      intercepts the simple overloads; every call here passes a CommandDefinition, because
      that is what carries the CancellationToken, and those are skipped silently — no
      interceptor was emitted anywhere in the assembly. Cancellation is a correctness property
      and native compilation is a packaging convenience, so the package went rather than the
      tokens. Self-contained publish gives runtime independence without constraining how
      queries are written.

      Removing it did cost something, and less than it appears. The package also ships
      build-time SQL analyzers, so those went too — but they are built on a T-SQL parser, and
      nothing here ever declared the SQLite dialect, so what they were validating was this
      project's SQL read as somebody else's language. STRICT tables, RETURNING, json_valid,
      partial indexes and GLOB are all outside what that parser describes. Anyone reinstating
      the package should set the dialect first and expect to see diagnostics that were
      previously silent.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Dapper" Version="2.1.79" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The MCP server SDK, and the hosting it expects. ModelContextProtocol is the higher-level
    package: it brings the stdio transport, attribute-driven tool discovery, and the
    Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting integration that gives the tick loop somewhere to live.
    ModelContextProtocol.Core is the alternative and was considered - it has a smaller
    dependency graph, and would have meant hand-wiring the transport, the dispatch and a timer
    that the host otherwise provides. The graph is public, audited on every build, and the
    hosting packages ship with the runtime; the hand-wiring would have been ours to get wrong.
  -->
  <!--
    The transport. SPEC.md §4.0 chooses gRPC over HTTP/2, and what earns it is the held
    connection rather than the encoding: an agent's reachability is a fact about a socket, so
    the broker learns that a machine went away by the stream ending rather than by a timestamp
    ageing past a threshold somebody had to pick.

    Grpc.Tools is build-only. It runs protoc over proto/parley.proto and contributes nothing to
    the output graph, so PrivateAssets keeps it off every consumer's; the generated code depends
    on Google.Protobuf and Grpc.Net.Client, which are real references and are declared as such.

    Grpc.AspNetCore is referenced only by the process that hosts the broker. It brings Kestrel,
    and an MCP server speaking over stdio has no business carrying a web server: the contract
    assembly is separate from the host precisely so a client can reference the first without
    acquiring the second.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Transport">
    <PackageVersion Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.36.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Net.Client" Version="2.83.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Tools" Version="2.83.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" Version="2.83.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Adapters">
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol" Version="2.2.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The terminal surface of `parley top`, and the first dependency on the tool that is neither
    Microsoft's nor gRPC's.

    That is worth a sentence rather than a shrug, because NuGetAudit fails this build on a high or
    critical advisory and every package is a new place one can land. Spectre.Console earns it by
    replacing what the alternative would be: drawing a scrolling transcript, a live log tail and a
    selectable list with System.Console directly, which is how the startup banner is drawn and is
    the reason the banner is a banner. The fallback if this ever has to go is therefore a smaller
    feature rather than a different library.

    It brings nothing with it on .NET 10 — no transitive package dependencies at all, so there is
    no pin below and nothing new on the graph but the one assembly.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Terminal">
    <PackageVersion Include="Spectre.Console" Version="0.57.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    xUnit v3. The entire v2 package family (xunit, xunit.core, xunit.assert) is marked
    deprecated on NuGet: legacy, security fixes only, all feature work moved to v3. The
    package name carries the product version in v3 so the package version itself can follow
    SemVer, which is why xunit.v3 is at 4.x rather than 3.x.

    Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and xunit.runner.visualstudio are deliberately absent. Both belong
    to the VSTest era, and the .NET 10 SDK no longer runs Microsoft.Testing.Platform tests
    through the VSTest target. xunit.v3 hosts its own MTP runner, so the test projects need
    nothing else. The runner is selected in global.json, under a "test" section alongside
    "sdk" — not in dotnet.config, whose INI format was dropped during .NET 10 RC2.
  -->
  <!--
    Analyzers, not dependencies: PrivateAssets keeps them off every consumer's graph, and nothing
    they enforce reaches a shipped assembly.

    BannedApiAnalyzers exists for one symbol so far, and it earned its place. Several test classes
    called SqliteConnection.ClearAllPools() to release a file handle before reading or deleting a
    temporary database. That call is process-global, xUnit runs test classes in parallel, and
    under load it disposed the native handle of a connection another class was using,
    surfacing as "ObjectDisposedException: SQLitePCL.sqlite3" from whichever test happened to be mid-query.
    It presented as a flaky test failing about once in twenty-five runs of the suite, was not
    reproducible in ninety runs afterwards, and took a build running concurrently to bring back.

    The calls are gone. This is what stops the next one being written, because the reason not to
    write it is invisible at the call site: the code looks local and is not.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Testing">
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Spectre.Console.Testing" Version="0.57.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" Version="4.14.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ==== Transitive pins ====

    Entries in the group below are NOT referenced directly by any project. They exist to
    raise the resolved version of a package that arrives through someone else's dependency
    graph, almost always to clear a security advisory.

    Every entry must record why it exists and when it can be removed. A pin with no
    justification becomes permanent by default, and the project silently accumulates version
    floors that nobody dares touch. Remove a pin once the parent package's own floor has
    caught up; listing transitive packages will show whether it still binds.

    Format for each entry, as a comment directly above it:
      reason:  NU1903 (high) via Parent.Package 4.5.6, advisory GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
      remove:  when Parent.Package reaches 4.6.0, which floors it itself

    Packages that ship as a matched set and can only move together take one shared entry
    above the group rather than one apiece. Three copies of a justification drift apart, and
    the first one edited becomes the one that is wrong.

    NOTE: a double hyphen cannot appear anywhere inside an XML comment. Write CLI flags in
    docs/ or the README instead of here, or MSBuild will reject this whole file as invalid
    with an error that does not say why.

    NOTE: SQLitePCLRaw renamed and restructured its native payload between major lines, so
    the package that carries the engine is not the same package on both. On the 2.x line the
    engine is SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3, versioned independently of the SQLite inside it
    (package 2.1.12 ships engine 3.53.3). On the 3.x line bundle_e_sqlite3 no longer references
    that package at all: it depends on SQLitePCLRaw.config.e_sqlite3 and on a package named
    plainly SQLite, versioned as the engine is. Pinning lib.e_sqlite3 alongside a 3.x bundle
    therefore resolves a package that nothing loads, and the pin reads as effective while
    changing nothing. Confirm which package supplies the engine before pinning, and confirm
    the engine itself with sqlite_version() rather than by reading version numbers.

    NOTE: verify a pin by restoring before you read the graph. Editing this file and running
    a query against the dependency graph in the same breath reports the previous restore's
    answer, which will agree with whatever you just changed and tell you nothing.
  -->

  <!--
    reason:  moves the engine off the 2.x line, whose only advisory to date, GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q
             (high), is recorded with no patched version. Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.11 floors
             SQLitePCLRaw at 2.1.12, one release above the affected range, so the graph is clean
             and this is not a fix for a live finding. It is a move off a line where a future
             engine advisory has nowhere published to go. On 3.x the affected package leaves the
             graph entirely, replaced by SQLite 3.53.4, which is also the newer engine.
    remove:  when Microsoft.Data.Sqlite floors SQLitePCLRaw.core at 3.x itself. Its 10.0.11
             nuspec still declares 2.1.12, so these three pins are what carry the whole family
             across together. They are a matched set and must move as one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Transitive pins">
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.core" Version="3.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.provider.e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
